    "title": "Nonlinear Programming Memo (I): Unconstrained Optimization",
    "date": "Sun Sep 28 2025 08:00:00 GMT+0800 (GMT+08:00)",
    "excerpt": "Recording some knowledge from the optimization learning process. I find that these details tend to be forgotten after a while, and different sources and books often present them inconsistently. This post aims to consolidate them. 1. Step Size Selection (1) Trivial Step Sizes: Constant step size—chosen empirically and dependent on initialization; too large causes oscillation and divergence, too small converges very slowly, and only appropriate values achieve fast and stable convergence. In short, not very intelligent. Diminishing step size—widely applicable, especially for non-smooth functions or noisy gradients.",
