    "title": "What Was Laplace's Original Purpose in Introducing the Laplace Transform?",
    "date": "Mon Jul 24 2023 08:00:00 GMT+0800 (GMT+08:00)",
    "excerpt": "In the 18th century, integral transforms were commonly used to solve differential equations. Euler first derived ∫X(x)eᵃˣdx, Lagrange derived ∫X(x)e⁻ᵃˣaˣdx, and Laplace in 1785 derived ∫xˢφ(x)dx. At the time, concepts like function spaces did not exist; people simply applied these transforms to differential equations without deeper reflection. After Fourier introduced his transform in the early 19th century, Lapl...",
    "tags": [
      "Zhihu Archive",
      "Zhihu Answer"
    ],
    "source": "https://www.zhihu.com/question/279486395/answer/3133750919",
    "sourceType": "zhihu-answer",
    "zhihuId": "3133750919",
    "translationStatus": "translated",
    "content": "\r\nIn the 18th century, it was common practice to solve differential equations using [integral transforms](https://zhida.zhihu.com/search?content_id=599857471&content_type=Answer&match_order=1&q=%E7%A7%AF%E5%88%86%E5%8F%98%E6%8D%A2&zhida_source=entity).\r\n\r\nEuler was the first to derive ∫X(x)eᵃˣdx, Lagrange followed with ∫X(x)e⁻ᵃˣaˣdx, and Laplace introduced ∫xˢφ(x)dx in 1785. At the time, however, there was no notion of function spaces—these tools were applied to differential equations naively, without deeper reflection.\r\n\r\nIt was only after Fourier proposed his transform in the early 19th century that Laplace came to reassess his earlier work. Later, it became clear that *s* could be interpreted as an extension of frequency into the complex domain.\r\n"
